WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.497 --> 00:00:03.247
(rhythmic music)

00:00:04.913 --> 00:00:07.310
(train horn)

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:08.630
- I feel like that saying if they say,

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:11.200
you can make it in New York,
you can make it anywhere.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.613
I am from Brooklyn, so I
feel like I can do anything.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.607
My name is Carter Clinton, and
I'm a genetic anthropologist

00:00:18.607 --> 00:00:20.503
and a National Geographic Explorer.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.464
Presently, one of the major issues

00:00:23.464 --> 00:00:25.450
that we find in genomic research,

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.233
is the lack of representation
for African Americans.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:32.280
Genomic research is the
study of all of your genes

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.260
at a basic level.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.830
And one of the major goals of this project

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.570
was to contribute
information from a historic,

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.223
African American population.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.700
Soil has a lot of secrets

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.980
that you can't see with the naked eye.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.780
I'm studying 400 year old grave soil

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.010
to learn more about the
health and lifestyles

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.343
of a historic African American population.

00:01:00.472 --> 00:01:02.805
(footsteps)

00:01:03.927 --> 00:01:06.677
(oriental music)

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.830
So this is the New York
African Burial Ground.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:14.399
It's now a national monument.

00:01:14.399 --> 00:01:15.770
(oriental music)

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.770
In 1991, a construction
crew attempted to erect

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.830
a federal building at 290 Broadway.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.830
Being one of the taller
buildings in the area,

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:27.560
it was about 30 stories high,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.910
they had to dig 30 feet below.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.140
And around 25 feet

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.263
they began to uncover and
unearth human remains.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.480
After they found the human remains,

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.062
they went back to the historical maps,

00:01:43.062 --> 00:01:46.020
and they realized that
they had just rediscovered

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.993
the New York African Burial Ground.

00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:52.380
419 were excavated,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.900
and that's out of an
estimated 15,000 individuals,

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.793
over 6.6 acres of land.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.290
So here we have

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.980
the New York African Burial
Ground Grave Soil Collection,

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.850
housed at Howard University.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.470
And each of the samples
represents an individual

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.440
who was buried 400 years ago,

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.313
and each sample is collected
from a specific body region,

00:02:15.313 --> 00:02:18.103
from each of those individuals.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:24.910
So we each have our own
unique bacterial community,

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:29.910
or signature, if you will, of
bacteria that live within us,

00:02:30.458 --> 00:02:33.433
on us, like on our skin, and our gut.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.030
When we die, that bacteria
is the leading force

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:42.083
in how our bodies are decomposed.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.160
So what we're looking at is what survives

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.353
after 400 years after a
person has been buried.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.060
So we took the original soil samples,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.060
we sieved it, we collected
those fine particles,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.773
and then we attempted a DNA extraction.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:06.010
So we did that hundreds of times,

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.433
over the span of about six months.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:13.253
And then one day, we actually got results.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.170
It was the best day ever,

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.683
it was the best day ever.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.670
So, even hundreds of years
after a person passes away,

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:26.910
you can see infectious disease bacteria

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:30.163
that may be the result of
what that person died from.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:34.779
(running water)

00:03:34.779 --> 00:03:37.446
(piano playing)

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:39.900
I feel proud.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.062
I feel proud to be an African American

00:03:43.062 --> 00:03:48.062
working on what could possibly
be my direct ancestors.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:52.650
Being an African American
and native New Yorker

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.250
at the same time.

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:59.250
It's so much more than just the tangible.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.580
It's the knowledge that you had ancestors

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:02.970
who lived years ago,

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:06.292
that contributed to this greater picture

00:04:06.292 --> 00:04:08.243
that you're now a part of.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.140
So we're not just contributing to science,

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.640
we're contributing to history.

